two of the world's biggest tech  companies are facing diverging fortunes  just one day after facebook parent  meta's valuation fell by a record 237  billion dollars amazon is on track to  gain almost as much in market value  thanks to a nearly 20 stock rally in  after-hours trading that's after the  e-commerce giant set its profit doubled  from a year earlier to 14.3 billion  dollars the earnings rise comes just as  amazon prepares to hike its prime  subscription prices in the u.s  amazon surge bucks a trend seen in the  wake of metas fall where a number of  tech stocks saw heavy losses  now let's get more on this from our  reporter rob watts who joins me in the  studio  so rob we've seen really uh  big big differences in the performances  of facebook amazon what's behind that  yeah it's a real tale of two tech giants  amazon has got its own problems you know  problems that are being seen by all  sorts of companies you know supply chain  problems and such and actually amazon is  also seeing its growth slowing you know  it did really well at the height of the  pandemic when people were locked in at  home and you know what they were doing  was just ordering offline and getting  stuff sent to them but since people have  been out about once again amazon started  to see that slow down but it's made up  for any pitfalls with really good  performances in other parts of its  business for example its ad sales have  risen by about a third during the last  quarter  what amazon does it allows brands to pay  extra so you can see their products  first when you search on amazon's  website it's also seen as cloud  computing business do very well that's  grown by 40  during the last quarter and that's  really one of the big engines of new  growth for amazon it's behind you know  netflix's website mcdonald's website  they're all storing their data on  amazon's servers so that's all making up  for the the real problems that we're  seeing at amazon in terms of its growth  slowing so amazon also was preparing to  hike the the subscription price for  prime what is driving that decision  well i mentioned the challenges that  amazon is facing and it's really trying  to spend its way out of those problems  for example there's a real recruitment  squeeze in the united states companies  are struggling to get people amazon's  laid its money down and it's been  offering signing on bonuses to try and  get people to come and work for it also  those supply chain problems that i'm  talking about i mean that delivering  goods to people  at the sort of pace that people expect  from amazon has been tricky and it's  been spending more on deliveries to try  and make up for that problem and um  to take on the extra costs that it's  facing it's now looking to its millions  of prime subscribers and it's increasing  prices just in the united states an  extra two dollars a month or an extra 20  dollars per year and it's hoping by  doing that that it can start to claw  back the extra money that it's having to  spend elsewhere and amazon's raised its  prices before it raised them four years  ago and the four years ago before that  it didn't see too big a drop off in  subscriptions as a result of that and  it's hoping that this time you know  overall it will pay off well investors  obviously seem quite confident in this  strategy with amazon basically on track  to gain a fortune today  facebook other tech companies not doing  as well does it still make sense to  refer to all of these companies as big  tech is there really a diverging  field here well i think you group big  tech at your peril and i think the  fortunes of meta and amazon over the  past two days have really shown that  it's the differences in how they operate  that makes the difference it gives one  of them a soaring share price and the  other one sees it absolutely plummeting  but we've had another good example of it  as well over the past couple of days so  snap which owns snapchat that messaging  service where the message disappears  after a certain amount of time well when  facebook posted these disappointing  results or rather meta did  shares in  snap also took a hit people thought well  if if it's bad news for facebook it's  probably bad news for snap as well their  social media but then snap came out with  its first ever quarterly profit and  after that it shares soared by 58  because it's it's it's different to  facebook it isn't facebook so in all the  areas where facebook's struggling snap  is seeing its users grow it's also not  getting the tick tock effect that  facebook's getting particularly in in  india where tick tock you know is banned  snap is growing really fast so that's an  example of how you can't group even  social media together and so you  certainly can't group all big tech  that's rob watts from uw venus thank you  so much for being here  you
 

